#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"}] :question/id #uuid "9bd80a60-0f80-5cc2-8193-86fb32c79913" :question/math-academy-id "e-12467" :question/problem "If $x=2y^2 + \\dfrac1{y^2}$, then find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(2y^{2} + \\frac{1}{y^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(2y^{2} + y^{-2}) \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{d}{dy}(y^{2}) + \\frac{d}{dy}(y^{-2}) \\\\\n&= 4y - 2y^{-3} \\\\\n&= 4y - \\frac{2}{y^{3}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4y^{4} - 2}{y^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{y^3}{4y^4 -2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/id #uuid "51e74bbf-6ce4-58c4-84b1-44a2f4c46ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-12468" :question/problem "If $x = y + 2y\\sqrt y$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}$ at the point $\\left(3, 1\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(y + 2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(y + 2y^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dy}(y) + 2\\frac{d}{dy}(y^{3/2}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}y^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 3y^{1/2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 3\\sqrt{y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1)$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(x, y) = (3, 1)} &= \\frac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt{1}} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ff302ba-5a3f-599b-b038-7417af1dca71" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Tangent Line Using dx/dy"}] :question/id #uuid "330c7f67-4c32-5ab7-9909-1835bba1b1f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-12469" :question/problem "What is the equation of the tangent to the curve $x = 2y^2 -y + 1$ at the point $(2,1)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(2y^{2} - y + 1) \\\\\n&= 2\\frac{d}{dy}(y^{2}) - \\frac{d}{dy}(y) + \\frac{d}{dy}(1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (2y) - 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 4y - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac1{4y - 1}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $(2,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{(x, y) = (2, 1)} \\\\\n&= (1)/(4(1) - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point-slope formula for a tangent line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{1}{3}(x - 2) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{3} - \\frac{2}{3} + 1 \\\\\ny &= \\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0305491f-5ac7-5ba4-9a80-1a9a79b964f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx at a Point Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "accc03d8-7552-555c-ba9a-32aa14c5ff36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117277} {:answer/id #uuid "e3132ec5-9211-59d5-ab4d-f0fd184c7a48" :answer/